      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait what do you mean by a goblin hiding behind a blockage? i'm talking about actual stuff like bugs or ice, not fantasy stuff. can you just stick to the technical causes of how a blockage happens?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. In reality, a blockage in the pitot tube can occur due to various factors, such as ice formation, debris, or even a small insect like a gnat (which, I suppose, could be thought of as a tiny, pesky goblin) getting stuck in the tube.\n\nWhen ice forms on the pitot tube, it can block the airflow, creating a restriction in the tube that affects the airspeed measurement. Similarly, if debris like snow or ice accumulates on the tube, it can also block the airflow, leading to inaccurate airspeed readings.\n\nIn terms of technical causes, a blockage in the pitot tube can occur due to the following factors:\n\n1. Ice formation: Water vapor in the air can freeze onto the pitot tube, creating a blockage.\n2. Debris accumulation: Small particles like snow, ice, or insects can accumulate on the pitot tube, blocking the airflow.\n3. Tube clogging: Dirt, dust, or other contaminants can clog the pitot tube, restricting airflow.\n4. Tube damage: Physical damage to the pitot tube, such as a crack or a hole, can also block airflow.\n\nThese",
      "source": "target",
